{#
  Why goods whose expiry date is already past, or closer than the minimum, are being accepted
  (items 68, 69).

  One partial, included from every place an expiry box is rendered, so the control cannot differ
  between the two branches of `_receive_row.html.twig` the way the batch and serial boxes once did.

  It is a text box and not a checkbox on purpose. A tick records that somebody clicked; the only
  question asked about such a delivery a fortnight later is on what grounds it was taken, and a tick
  answers none of it. ReceivingService refuses a line with this blank when its date is already past
  OR inside the minimum shelf life, and records it as a `procurement_short_dated_receipt` row — with
  this text, the person, and the figures — when it is not. ONE box for both findings, because it is
  one decision about one pallet.

  No `required` attribute, for the same reason nothing else on this form has one: the row may not be
  being received at all, and `required` cannot say "only if" without JavaScript.
#}
<br><input type="text" id="{{ idx }}-short-dated-reason" name="{{ idx }}[short_dated_reason]" maxlength="255"
           value="{{ row.short_dated_reason|default('') }}" placeholder="Reason, if expired or short-dated">
<br><small>
    Only needed if the expiry above is <strong>already past</strong>, or closer than the minimum shelf life
    &mdash; you will be told which, and nothing is booked in until then. Recorded against the receipt with your
    name on it.
</small>
